  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Given the elliptic cone $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2},$ find its intercepts with the $y$-axis.",
  "worked_solution": "To find the $y$-intercepts of the elliptic cone, we plug $x=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{0}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2} \\\\ 0+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intercept with the $y$-axis is $(0,0,0)$ only.",
